  "id": "COMPOUND:9917 - PROTEIN:5829",
  "verbalization": "Osugacestat is implicated in the regulation of CHP-134 through multiple distinct biological pathways. The drug is shown to target T-cell surface glycoprotein CD3, which is a key component of the T-cell receptor complex, and is in turn targeted by two different molecules: Opposit-4(15)-Ene-1Beta,11-Diol and Caragasinin B. These two targets are both involved in the modulation of immune responses, suggesting that Osugacestat may influence immune cell function through the CD3 pathway. Additionally, Osugacestat is also shown to interact with UDP-glucuronosyltransferase 2A1, which is a cytochrome P450 enzyme involved in drug metabolism and detoxification. The interaction with this enzyme may indirectly affect CHP-134 through its downstream targets. The integrated evidence suggests that Osugacestat may exert its effects on CHP-134 through multiple mechanisms, primarily involving immune-related pathways and enzyme regulation. The presence of multiple targets and pathways supports a mechanistic link between Osugacestat and CHP-134, though further validation is required to confirm the direct interaction and functional relevance.",
